<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Law of Peace</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gayanashagowa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Law of Peace is the oral constitution of the Haudenosaunee, or Iroquois Confederacy. Tradition credits it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">the Peacemaker</a>, Deganawida, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a>, who persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, Cayuga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> to end their feuds and join a league, usually dated to the 15th century. It set out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> of chiefs and the role of clan mothers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace.jpg" alt="Great Law of Peace" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SargassoShearwater · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hiawatha_Belt_vector.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Great Law is set within a narrative that also prescribes laws and ceremonies to be performed at fixed times. Its laws are divided into 117 articles. The member nations are symbolised by an eastern white pine, the Tree of Peace, and each nation has a defined role in the conduct of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a>. The law was first recorded and passed on through wampum, belts of shell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> whose sequences of pictograms served storytellers as aids to memory.</p><p>The articles range widely. One provides for a war chief from each nation who carries messages for the chiefs, takes up arms in an emergency and conveys the warnings of the women to a chief who acts wrongly. Another declares that anyone who submits to the laws of a foreign people forfeits all claim in the Five Nations. Others name the festivals of thanksgiving, from the Midwinter Thanksgiving to the Great Festival of Ripe Corn, to be held in each nation's longhouses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The narrative exists in the languages of the member nations, so spellings and details differ. The scholar Christopher Vecsey identified 22 points shared across some two dozen versions, from the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">Deganawida</a> and the mourning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a> to the founding of the council and the Condolence ceremony for a dead chief. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> versions were published by Horatio Hale in 1883 and by Arthur C. Parker in 1910, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a> ones by John Arthur Gibson from 1899, and the Tuscarora, who joined the confederacy in 1722, have their own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Donald Grinde and Bruce Johansen argued that the confederacy's example inspired the framers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, a thesis that led Congress to pass Concurrent Resolution 331 in 1988 recognising such an influence. Many scholars dispute it. Elisabeth Tooker pointed out that the council decided by consensus of male chiefs chosen through descent and by their female relatives, and Jack N. Rakove found no trace of such influence in the records of the constitutional debates.</p><p>The principle that decisions should consider the seventh generation to come is often said to derive from the Great Law. Accounts of the law stress thinking of future generations, though they make no specific reference to seven, and the idea has been set out in a Haudenosaunee context by the Onondaga Faithkeeper Oren Lyons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Law of Peace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Together they persuaded the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca to accept the <b>Great Law of Peace</b>, the Gayanashagowa, and finally won over the feared Onondaga chief Tadodaho, whose name became the title of the league’s presiding chief.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With Hiawatha and Jigonhsasee, the Mother of Nations, whose home served as a kind of United Nations, he brought the <b>Great Law of Peace</b> to nations that were fighting and feuding with one another.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Law of Peace</b>, as later written down, names one war chief for each nation, to carry messages for the chiefs, take up arms in an emergency, watch the council’s proceedings and bring the complaints of the people and the warnings of the women to a chief…</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Haudenosaunee tradition they accepted the <b>Great Law of Peace</b> in the mid-15th century, joining the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga and Seneca in the Iroquois Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/cayuga-nation">Cayuga Nation</a></li><li><blockquote>Haudenosaunee tradition holds that they were the first nation to accept the Peacemaker's <b>Great Law of Peace</b>, around the mid-15th century, and so hold an elder place in the Iroquois Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around the mid-15th century, according to Haudenosaunee tradition, they joined the Mohawk, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca in accepting the <b>Great Law of Peace</b>, forming the Iroquois Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/oneida-nation">Oneida Nation</a></li></ul></section>
